    slug: "term-life-insurance-leads",
    title: "Term Life Insurance Leads: A Guide for Agents",
    metaTitle: "Term Life Insurance Leads Guide",
    excerpt: "A guide to sourcing and converting leads specifically for term life insurance products.",
    metaDescription: "Term life leads convert differently than whole life or final expense leads. Learn how agents source and work leads specifically for term life insurance products.",
    category: "Insurance",
    readTime: "7 min read",
    publishedDate: "2027-01-01",
    updatedDate: "2026-08-05",
    relatedSlugs: buyLeadsRel,
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Term life insurance leads connect agents with consumers seeking temporary coverage for a defined period, typically at lower premiums than permanent life insurance products." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This category often appeals to younger families seeking affordable coverage during specific financial obligation periods, such as while raising children or paying down a mortgage, rather than lifelong coverage with a savings component." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Understanding This Distinct Product Category" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Term life provides coverage for a set period, such as 20 or 30 years, without the cash value accumulation found in permanent life insurance. Because there's no investment component, term policies are generally simpler to underwrite and quote, which also makes this category well suited to fast, digital-first lead flows." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Common Triggers for Term Life Shopping" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "New mortgages, growing families, and income replacement needs during working years commonly trigger genuine term life shopping behavior. A new parent calculating what their family would need to replace their income for the next two decades is a classic, high-intent term life prospect." },
      { type: "heading", text: "How Pricing Works for Term Life Leads" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Lead pricing in this category typically reflects age, health class, and coverage amount requested. Younger, healthier prospects requesting standard coverage amounts are generally cheaper to acquire but also carry lower average commission per policy, while older prospects or those requesting larger face amounts often cost more per lead but can produce meaningfully higher commission. Leads that have already answered basic health questions, such as tobacco use and major conditions, tend to command a premium over unqualified data because they let agents skip straight to a real quote conversation." },
      { type: "heading", text: "What Defines a Quality Term Life Lead" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Genuine coverage interest confirmed near the time of sale.",
        "Confirmed general age and basic health information, including tobacco use.",
        "Accurate, reachable contact information with a working phone number.",
        "Documented consent for agent contact covering the intended outreach method.",
      ]},
      { type: "heading", text: "How to Evaluate a Lead Provider" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Ask whether leads include basic health prequalification or are purely demographic.",
        "Confirm exclusivity terms and how many agents receive the same lead.",
        "Request sample consent documentation showing how contact permission was captured.",
        "Clarify the provider's carrier appointment coverage relative to your own.",
      ]},
      { type: "heading", text: "Red Flags to Watch For" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Leads with no health prequalification sold at premium data-lead pricing.",
        "No disclosure of how many other agents received the same lead.",
        "Contact information that repeatedly bounces or goes to disconnected numbers.",
        "Vague or missing consent documentation for agent contact.",
      ]},
      { type: "heading", text: "Explaining Term Versus Permanent Coverage" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Agents who clearly explain the trade-offs between term and permanent coverage help prospects make more confident, informed decisions, particularly prospects who arrived already unsure which product actually fits their situation." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Simplified Issue Versus Fully Underwritten Term Leads" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Not all term life leads should be worked the same way. Prospects open to a simplified-issue policy, which skips a medical exam in exchange for a lower coverage cap and often a slightly higher rate, can often be closed the same day, while prospects seeking maximum coverage at the lowest rate typically need to go through full underwriting with a medical exam and lab work. Agents who ask early which path a prospect actually needs can avoid mismatched expectations about how quickly a policy will be issued." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Handling Health Disclosures Honestly During the Sales Process" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Term life sales built on incomplete health disclosures tend to unravel during underwriting, resulting in a declined application, a higher premium than initially quoted, or a rescinded policy after a claim. Agents who ask direct, specific health questions upfront, rather than letting a prospect self-select into a rate class that turns out to be inaccurate, close fewer initial sales but retain far more issued, in-force policies." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Sourcing Through a Trusted Marketplace" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Agents can source term life insurance leads through [Eilite's buy leads platform](/buy-leads) alongside other life insurance formats." },
      { type: "heading", text: "ROI and Cost-Per-Issued-Policy Framing" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Tracking cost per issued policy, rather than cost per lead alone, helps agents confirm their lead sourcing is genuinely producing strong returns, since a cheaper lead that rarely reaches underwriting approval can end up costing more per issued policy than a pricier, better-qualified one." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Agents who understand a family's specific financial obligation timeline tend to recommend more appropriately sized term lengths and coverage amounts." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "How does term life lead pricing compare to final expense or whole life leads?", a: "Term life leads often skew toward younger, healthier prospects and can be less expensive per lead, but that varies by requested coverage amount and health class. Final expense leads typically target older prospects with a narrower, more predictable product need." },
      { q: "What health information should a quality term life lead include?", a: "At minimum, basic tobacco use status and any major diagnosed conditions, since these significantly affect quoted premiums. Leads with this prequalification let agents skip straight to a real quote rather than starting from scratch." },
      { q: "Does lead exclusivity matter for term life insurance?", a: "Yes. Exclusive leads sold to a single agent generally convert at higher rates than shared leads distributed to multiple agents simultaneously, since prospects contacted by several agents at once often move forward with whichever responds first." },
      { q: "How long does it typically take to convert a term life lead into an issued policy?", a: "Timelines vary by underwriting requirements, but simplified-issue term products can sometimes bind within days, while fully underwritten policies requiring medical exams can take several weeks from initial contact to issued policy." },
      { q: "What's the best way to measure whether a term life lead source is worth the price?", a: "Track cost per issued policy rather than cost per lead alone, since conversion rates from lead to bound policy vary meaningfully by source and can make a pricier, better-qualified lead the more economical choice overall." },
      { q: "What's the difference between simplified issue and fully underwritten term life?", a: "Simplified issue skips a medical exam in exchange for a lower coverage cap and often a slightly higher rate, and can often be issued the same day. Fully underwritten policies require an exam and lab work but generally offer higher coverage amounts at lower rates." },
    ],
